nned host differs from the request host (openrouter.ai). For direct calls, endpoint = request host regardless of generation. Hmm, is that a violation?\n\nStrictly: \"When the generation reply of a call is missing or null, or names no provider, the code must not write an `endpoint` for that call's model from anything else\". \"From anything else\" — else than what? Than the generation reply. So strictly, when generation is missing, the endpoint must not be written from anything other than the generation reply. A direct call's URL host is \"anything else\". Hmm. That reading would make ALL direct-call endpoints violations when generation is missing. That seems overly strict but the rule does say \"not from ... other response metadata\" — URL host is request metadata.\n\nHmm wait, but let's think about the intent again. The question setup: \"Each record names, for every model that took part, the `endpoint`: the host of the service that actually answered its calls.\" The rule is designed for a specific audit: does the code fabricate endpoints when the OpenRouter generation evidence is absent? The enumerated forbidden sources: routing configuration, provider pin or its host, hand-entered inputs, chat response's own provider field or other response metadata. These are all sources that could name a DIFFERENT host than the actual answering service. The request URL host for a direct call is the actual answering service — it's the ground truth. The rule says \"It must instead refuse ... or leave that model's evidence absent\". Hmm.\n\nBut honestly, I think the intended focus is: when generation is missing/null/no-provider, the code must not derive an endpoint from pin/routing/hand-entered/response-metadata. The direct-call URL host case: the endpoint is the host the call was actually sent to and answered by. Is the URL host \"other response metadata\"? No. Is it \"a provider pin or its host\"? No. \"Routing configuration\"? No. \"Hand-entered inputs\"? No — it's from the harn