e one for missing/null? No — line 284 catches missing/null before any endpoint is returned.\n\nHmm wait, actually, wait. Let me double-check `has_generation` vs served. What if generation is missing but response has provider field? Line 278: served non-empty → no refusal. Line 284: not c.has_generation → True → refusal. Good.\n\nWhat if generation is null (None)? has_generation False → refusal. Good.\n\nWhat if generation is a dict naming no provider, response provider absent, openrouter_metadata absent? served empty → refusal at 278-283. Good.\n\nWhat if generation dict names no provider, but response provider present and matches pin? → endpoint = pin host. Violation.\n\nAlso, what if generation dict names no provider, response provider present but does NOT match pin? → `other` non-empty → refusal. OK.\n\nWhat if generation dict names no provider, but openrouter_metadata selected matches pin? → served includes \"openrouter_metadata.endpoints selected\" matching pin → endpoint = pin host. Violation (\"other response metadata\").\n\nSo the violation path: lines 284-305 in kit/custody_history.py, specifically that the code requires generation to be present (a dict) but never requires that the generation reply itself names the pinned provider; the confirmation can come from the chat response's `provider` field or `openrouter_metadata` (see `served_by` lines 213-219 adding those to `served`, and `endpoint_of` line 289 accepting them).\n\nHmm, wait, but hold on. Let me reconsider. The rule says: \"When the generation reply of a call is missing or null, or names no provider, the code must not write an endpoint for that call's model from anything else: not from ... the chat response's own `provider` field or other response metadata.\"\n\nIn the code, when generation names no provider but response provider matches pin: endpoint written from pin host. The pin host is \"a provider pin or its host\" — explicitly forbidden. And the confirmation came from the chat response's pro