data\") if isinstance(g.get(\"data\"), dict) else g\n        if g.get(\"provider_name\"):\n            served[\"generation provider_name\"] = str(g[\"provider_name\"])\n        pr = [...]\n    return served, tried\n```\n\nYes. So served can be populated by response provider / openrouter_metadata without any generation provider_name.\n\nAnd `endpoint_of`:\n\n```python\n    route = {\"aggregator\": c.host, \"upstream_provider\": c.provider}\n    if not pin: refusal\n    if not c.served: refusal\n    if not c.has_generation: refusal\n    other = {src: n for src, n in c.served.items() if not pin_matches(n, pin)}\n    if other: refusal\n    tried = ...; if tried: refusal\n    route.update({... endpoint_basis: f\"declared pin ... served-by confirmed from {', '.join(sorted(c.served))}\"})\n    return pin.get(\"host\"), route\n```\n\nSo the endpoint returned is pin host; the confirmation set `c.served` can include \"response provider\" and \"openrouter_metadata\" entries. If generation names no provider but response provider matches pin → endpoint = pin host. VIOLATION per rule.\n\nAlso consider: generation dict names pin provider AND response provider names a different provider → `other` non-empty → refusal. Fine.\n\nGeneration dict names pin provider, response provider matches pin too → endpoint = pin host. Allowed (generation reply is non-null and names the provider; mapping to pinned host is not a violation).\n\nSo the sole violating path: generation present-but-provider-less + response metadata (provider field or openrouter_metadata) matching pin → endpoint written from pin.\n\nHmm, but hold on — is that truly \"the generation reply ... names no provider\"? Yes. And the rule says in that case the code must not write the endpoint from the chat response's provider field or other response metadata (nor pin host). The code does. Violation.\n\nWait, but I want to double-check the `has_generation` check once more. `self.has_generation = isinstance(raw.get(\"generation\"), di